Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Implementation Opinions on Reforming and Improving the Judicial Committee System of People's Courts

Fa Fa [2010] No. 3

To: the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Implementation Opinions on Reforming and Improving the Judicial Committee System of People's Courts have been approved by the Central Authorities. We hereby issue them to you for earnest implementation. If you have any opinions or suggestions during implementation, please promptly report them to our Court.

January 11, 2010

Implementation Opinions on Reforming and Improving the Judicial Committee System of People's Courts

To reform and improve the Judicial Committee system of People's Courts, enhance the quality and efficiency of adjudication work, and in accordance with the provisions of the Organic Law of the People's Courts, the Criminal Procedure Law, the Civil Procedure Law, the Administrative Procedure Law, and other laws, and in light of the actual adjudication work of the People's Courts, these Opinions are formulated.

1. The Judicial Committee system of the People's Courts is an important component of the socialist judicial system with Chinese characteristics. Over the past decades, the Judicial Committees of People's Courts at all levels have played a significant role in summing up adjudication experience, guiding adjudication work, and adjudicating difficult, complex, and major cases. With the development of China's socialist market economy and the construction of democracy and the legal system, the awareness of the public in resolving disputes through the courts has continuously strengthened. The total number of cases accepted by courts nationwide and the number of new types of cases have increased year by year, and the requirements for adjudication quality have become increasingly higher. To adapt to the requirements of the new situation and new tasks, establish a fair, efficient, and authoritative socialist judicial system, and achieve the scientization and standardization of the working mechanism and procedures of the Judicial Committee, the Judicial Committee system of the People's Courts should be continuously reformed and improved.

2. The reform and improvement of the Judicial Committee system shall adhere to the guiding ideology of "Three Supremes" in the work of the People's Courts, adhere to the Party's leadership over the work of the People's Courts, consciously accept the supervision of the People's Congresses, consciously uphold the dignity and authority of the Constitution and laws, consciously safeguard the legitimate rights and interests of the people, and adhere to proceeding from the actual adjudication work, advancing steadily and actively in accordance with the law.

3. The Judicial Committee is the highest adjudication organ of the People's Court and plays an important role in summing up adjudication experience and adjudicating difficult, complex, and major cases.

4. The Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court performs the duties of adjudicating cases, and supervising, managing, and guiding adjudication work:
(1) Discussing difficult, complex, and major cases;
(2) Summing up adjudication work experience;
(3) Formulating judicial interpretations and normative documents;
(4) Hearing work reports from adjudication business departments;
(5) Discussing and deciding on typical cases that have guiding significance for adjudication work;
(6) Discussing other major issues concerning adjudication work.

5. The Judicial Committees of local People's Courts at all levels perform the duties of adjudicating cases, and supervising, managing, and guiding adjudication work:
(1) Discussing difficult, complex, and major cases;
(2) Summing up adjudication work experience in light of the actual conditions of the region and the court;
(3) Hearing work reports from adjudication business departments;
(4) Discussing and deciding on cases that have reference significance for the adjudication work of the court or its jurisdiction;
(5) Discussing other major issues concerning adjudication work.

6. People's Courts at all levels shall strengthen the professional development of the Judicial Committee, improve the political quality, moral quality, and legal professional quality of its members, enhance judicial capability, and ensure that the members of the Judicial Committee become the judges with the best quality and highest level in the People's Court. In addition to the president, vice-presidents, and division chiefs serving as members of the Judicial Committee, People's Courts at all levels shall also equip a number of senior judge members who hold no leadership positions, have good political quality, rich adjudication experience, a relatively high level of legal theory, and possess higher education in law.

The Central Committee of the Communist Party of China's "Decision on Further Strengthening the Work of the People's Courts and People's Procuratorates" has already specified the staffing specifications and conditions for full-time members of the Judicial Committee. People's Courts at all levels shall equip a number of full-time members of the Judicial Committee.

7. Major issues in the adjudication work of the People's Court, difficult, complex, and major cases, and cases where the collegial panel finds it difficult to make a ruling shall be discussed or adjudicated by the Judicial Committee before a decision is made. Whether a case or issue is submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion shall be decided by the president or the vice-president in charge.

8. The following cases adjudicated by the Supreme People's Court shall be submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion and decision:
(1) Cases where the court's legally effective judgments or rulings are indeed erroneous and require retrial;
(2) Criminal cases where the Supreme People's Procuratorate has filed a protest under the trial supervision procedure.

9. The following cases adjudicated by the High People's Courts and Intermediate People's Courts shall be submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion and decision:
(1) Cases where the court's legally effective judgments or rulings are indeed erroneous and require retrial;
(2) Criminal cases where the people's procuratorate at the same level has filed a protest under the trial supervision procedure;
(3) Cases where the death penalty with immediate execution is proposed to be imposed;
(4) Cases where it is proposed to impose a penalty below the statutory punishment or to exempt from criminal punishment;
(5) Cases where it is proposed to declare the defendant not guilty;
(6) Cases where it is proposed to request instructions from a higher people's court on issues of legal application;
(7) Cases considered to be of major and complex nature, requiring a report and transfer to a higher people's court for adjudication.

10. The following cases adjudicated by the Basic People's Courts shall be submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion and decision:
(1) Cases where the court's legally effective judgments or rulings are indeed erroneous and require retrial;
(2) Cases where it is proposed to impose a penalty below the statutory punishment or to exempt from criminal punishment;
(3) Cases where it is proposed to declare the defendant not guilty;
(4) Cases where it is proposed to request instructions from a higher people's court on issues of legal application;
(5) Criminal cases considered to require a sentence of life imprisonment or death penalty, necessitating a report and transfer to an intermediate people's court for adjudication;
(6) Cases considered to be of major and complex nature, requiring a report and transfer to a higher people's court for adjudication.

11. When a People's Court adjudicates the following types of cases, the collegial panel may request the president to decide on submitting them to the Judicial Committee for discussion:
(1) Cases where there are significant disagreements among the collegial panel members, making it difficult to reach a decision;
(2) Cases where the legal provisions are unclear, presenting difficult issues in legal application;
(3) Cases where the outcome of the case may have a significant social impact;
(4) New types of cases that have guiding significance for adjudication work;
(5) Other difficult, complex, or major cases that need to be submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion.

For cases where the collegial panel has not suggested submitting them to the Judicial Committee for discussion, if the president, vice-president in charge, or division chief deems it necessary, they may submit the case to the Judicial Committee for discussion.

12. Cases that need to be submitted to the Judicial Committee for discussion shall be reported by the collegial panel through the division chief and vice-president in charge to the president for decision. If the president, vice-president in charge, or division chief considers that submission is unnecessary, they may request the collegial panel to reconsider.

When the Judicial Committee discusses a case, the collegial panel shall submit a case adjudication report. The case adjudication report shall conform to standard requirements, objectively and comprehensively reflect the case facts, evidence, and the opinions of both parties or the prosecution and defense, and explain the focus of disputes, differing opinions, and the proposed content of the adjudication. The case adjudication report shall be distributed to the members of the Judicial Committee in advance.

13. When the Judicial Committee discusses a case, all members of the collegial panel and the head of the adjudication business department shall attend the meeting as non-voting participants. If a retrial is initiated for a legally effective case concluded by the court, the members of the original collegial panel and the head of the adjudication business department shall also attend the meeting. The president or the vice-president authorized by the president to preside over the meeting may decide on other necessary attendees.

When the Judicial Committee discusses a case, the chief procurator of the people's procuratorate at the same level, or a deputy chief procurator authorized by the chief procurator, may attend as a non-voting participant.

14. The Judicial Committee meeting shall be presided over by the president. If the president cannot preside over the meeting due to some reason, a vice-president may be entrusted to preside.

15. The Judicial Committee discusses cases in the order of hearing reports, questioning, expressing opinions, and voting. The case shall be reported by the judge responsible for the case, with other members of the collegial panel supplementing. After the members of the Judicial Committee have heard the report, asked questions, and expressed their opinions, other non-voting participants may express opinions with the consent of the presiding officer.

16. The Judicial Committee discusses cases under the principle of democratic centralism. Generally, the order of expressing opinions by members of the Judicial Committee shall follow the principle that members with higher ranks speak later, with the presiding officer speaking last.

The Judicial Committee shall fully and comprehensively discuss the case. Members of the Judicial Committee shall express their opinions objectively, fairly, independently, and equally. The opinions expressed by members of the Judicial Committee shall not be subject to investigation, and shall be recorded in the files.

After the members of the Judicial Committee have expressed their opinions, the presiding officer shall summarize the members' opinions, draft a resolution based on the majority opinion, and put it to a vote. The resolution of the Judicial Committee shall be made by a majority of more than half of all members.

17. The Judicial Committee performs its duties of supervising, managing, and guiding adjudication work through meeting resolutions.

18. People's Courts at or above the intermediate level may establish a standing body for the Judicial Committee, and Basic People's Courts may designate full-time staff for the Judicial Committee.

The standing body for the Judicial Committee is responsible for handling the daily affairs of the Judicial Committee, supervising, inspecting, and implementing the decisions of the Judicial Committee, and undertaking other matters assigned by the Judicial Committee.
